  "query_preview": "(This evening through Wednesday)...\nIssued at 249 PM EDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nSurface Analysis this afternoon shows a broad surface trough \nstretching from central IL to across Central Indiana. Convection \ncontinued along this trough spreading showers and thunderstorms \nacross much of Central Indiana. Water vapor shows a steady NW flow \naloft in place, with an wave over IL, generating convection \ndownstream. Dewpoints remained high, in the 60s as much of the area \nwas under the influence of rain cooled air, however across southwest \nCentral Indiana, dew points were in the 70s, providing very humid \nconditions along with the best instability.\n\nThis afternoon through the overnight...\n\nThe Thunderstorm complex over Central Indiana will continue to \npropagate SE across Central Indiana through the late afternoon \nhours. Heavy rains may result in additional flooding and severe \nstorms cannot be ruled out. Near 00Z the HRRR progresses this system \nfurther SE, exiting Central Indiana as the forcing with upper wave \nexits. Clouds will remain across the area through the evening with \nrain cooled air in place. \n\nOvernight as the LLJ resumes, more storm development will be \npossible, but at this time HRRR is developing storms along the \nboundary which at that time should be located across southern \nCentral Indiana due to the southward push from today's convection. \nThus will focus best pops overnight in those locations. Fog will \nalso be possible overnight due to high dew points, light winds and \nplenty of lingering lower level moisture.\n\nThursday and Thursday Night...\n\nMore rain chances will be expected on Thursday afternoon and into \nThursday night. The persistent NW flow aloft is once again expected \nto continue across central Indiana. An upper level wave is expected \nto pass across central Indiana providing forcing through the \nafternoon hours. The location of the surface trough will be key in \nbest initiation and propagation area. For now, this is suggested to \nbe across southern Central Indiana, but changes can occur overnight. \nForecast soundings show shallower CAPE at that time with values only \naround 1000-1500 J/kg across Central Indiana, but much higher values \nsouth of boundary in the warmer and humid air mass. Thus will again \nuse high pops on Thursday afternoon for more showers and storm \ndevelopment given the familiar, favorable set-up in place. Again, \nbest chances for now will be across southern Central Indiana which \nis the expected location of the surface trough. Flash Flood Watch \nwill be extended.\n\nFriday and Friday Night...\n\nStrong ridging within the upper flow is expected to push east toward \nIndiana on Friday. This will allow surface warm front to surge \nacross Central Indiana as flow becomes southwesterly. Forecast \nsoundings again on Friday show a nearly saturated column at times, \nwith pwats once again over 2 inches. Again shallow CAPE appears \navailable. Thus again likely pops will be needed from the afternoon \nand into the evening as the warm front surges northeast across the \narea and acts as a focus for convection.\n\nSaturday through Sunday Night...\n\nRain chances will persist on Saturday and Sunday as a warm frontal \nboundary is suggested to be lingering across Central Indiana. \nWorking against this will be the pattern aloft where upper level \nridging will be building across Indiana from the west. This will \nresult in some subsidence within the mid and upper levels. However, \ndiurnal convection cannot be ruled out due to the frontal boundary \nthat will be present across the area on Saturday as a warm front and \non Sunday as a cold front crossing Indiana. SW flow ahead of the \ncold front will result in hot weather on Saturday and again on \nSunday with highs in the 90s.\n\nMonday through Wednesday...\n\nBetter chances for dry weather and temperatures closer to normal \nwill be expected during this time. Northwest flow is expected to \nresume across the area on Monday due to upper ridging in place over \nthe Rockies. Little in the way of forcing looks to pass during this \ntime. Meanwhile at the lower levels, stronger Canadian high pressure \nwill be building behind Sunday's cold front on Monday and through \nthe first part of the next work week. This should result in partly \ncloudy skies and highs in the 80s. Temps will moderate by Wednesday \nas the surface high drifts farther east and southerly return flow \nbegins.",
